  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 200 PM Friday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A cold front moving across central North Carolina \ntonight will bring light to moderate rain to all locations and \nsharply colder temperatures for Saturday.\n\nAlthough a band of convective showers could bring a brief period of \nprecipitation this evening, a more widespread area of rain will move \ninto central North Carolina late this evening into the overnight \nhours. Low pressure will develop along the front by Saturday \nmorning, bringing the chance of rain to near 100% from the Triangle \neast for Saturday morning. The heaviest rain is forecast to occur \nSaturday morning, but rain will linger east of the Triad into the \nafternoon. While Thursday night's low temperatures were generally in \nthe 60s, tonight's lows will be slightly colder, ranging from the \nupper 40s to the mid 50s. However, it will not be possible to step \noutside Saturday and not notice the difference in temperatures from \ntoday. One or two isolated locations may manage to reach the 60s, \nbut otherwise widespread 50s are forecast for Saturday's highs, with \nthe return of sunshine likely by sunset.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...  Patchy frost possible in the Piedmont Sunday \nmorning and Monday morning, followed by moderating but below-normal \ntemperatures for the workweek.\n\n\ufeffIn the wake of Saturday's cold front, a deep shortwave will swing \nacross the region and stall roughly along the Carolina coast through \nat least the middle of next week. A strong 1034 mb surface high will \nbuild eastward across the Midwest, positioning itself adjacent to a \ncoastal low that is expected to develop in response to the upper-\nlevel trough. This strong pressure gradient setup will maintain a \nsteady and at times breezy north-northeasterly flow early in the \nweek, along with a gradual increase in low-end rain chances each \nday, though exact details will depend heavily on the position and \nmovement of the upper low.\n\nThe airmass behind the front will support overnight lows in the 30s \nacross the Piedmont, with potential for radiational cooling into the \nlower 30s. However, the tighter pressure gradient should maintain at \nleast light stirring overnight Saturday and Sunday night, while high-\nlevel moisture wraps back around the upper low across central and \neastern NC. Consequently, while temperatures may warrant a Frost \nAdvisory with some temps in the mid-30s, it remains uncertain \nwhether winds will fully decouple to allow for more widespread \nimpacts.\n\nFor the remainder of the week, weather impacts will be closely tied \nto the position of the upper low. The low looks to remain cut off \nfrom the primary westerlies and may drift south-southwestward late \nin the week as ridging attempts to build overtop of it into the \ncentral Appalachians. Operational guidance (GFS/ECMWF) remains west \nof the AI-based counterparts, though all agreeing better with the \nGEFS mean than the eastern-outlying EPS mean. Thus, confidence in \nsensible weather impacts remains somewhat low. Expect a gradual \nmoderation in temperatures, though highs will remain below normal, \nalong with increasing cloud cover, particularly over eastern \ncounties. Latest guidance continues to support slight chance PoPs \neast of US-1 each day, which makes sense based on model trends and \nproximity of the upper low but overall weak forcing.",
